  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n - Thunderstorms this afternoon across western Montana and north \n   central Idaho. Main threats will be lightning and gusty outflow\n   winds. Thunderstorms again on Thursday, with geographical \n   focus more on southwest Montana.\n\n - A cold front will arrive late Friday into Saturday, bringing \n   gusty westerly winds, drier air, and elevated fire weather \n   conditions. \n\n- There is increasing confidence in a cooler, breezier pattern \n  this weekend\n\n\nA ridge of high pressure with its axis just east of the Northern \nRockies will draw moisture up from the southwest and into western\nMontana and north-central Idaho today. Hot, dry conditions will \npersist. Instability will increase with afternoon heating, \nallowing scattered, high-based showers and thunderstorms to \ndevelop. Model soundings indicate a dry lower atmosphere. As a \nresult, the primary threats from these storms will be strong, \nerratic outflow winds and lightning. Thunderstorm activity will \ninitiate over southwest Montana and central Idaho this afternoon \nbefore tracking northward toward the I-90 corridor and northwest \nMontana by this evening. The combination of mostly dry storms, \nlightning, and gusty winds will elevate the potential for new fire\nstarts. On Thursday, the high pressure ridge begins to break down\nas a trough of low pressure approaches from the west. Expect breezy\nconditions with another round of thunderstorms, mainly across \nsouthwest Montana. A subtle drop in temperatures arrives Friday \nas a cold front moves through the region. Gusty winds, dry \nconditions, and lingering thunderstorms will keep fire weather \nconditions elevated both days. \n\nThe weekend will trend significantly cooler with temperatures at \nor even slightly below normal. There are still a few models \nshowing a more significant low pressure system moving through \nnorthwest Montana, but the majority of models solutions are \ntrending more towards an open wave, which decreases the chance of \nprecipitation. There is still potential to see shower and \nthunderstorm activity in this pattern, but this would be more \nfocused across southwest Montana and Lemhi County.",
